  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n00Z KPBZ RAOB indicates a well-mixed boundary layer surmounted\nby a 200mb-deep dry layer from H8-H6. Atop this dry layer is a\nmoist-adiabatic saturated layer near H5, suggesting considerable\nwork still to do for any precipitation to reach the ground west\nof the mountains. \n\nPrior message of very low probability of rain west of I-79 axis\nseems reasonable. With the dry air in place, it remains to be \nseen how much rain will ultimately reach the ground ANYWHERE in\nthe forecast region west of the ridges (as some hi-res ensemble\nmembers suggest). If it does end up raining, it likely will be \nvery light and brief. Previous discussion follows...\n\nSunday and Monday will feature much more in the way of cloud \ncover than the past two days as mid and upper level moisture \nassociated with the Nor'easter moves westward. As of now, there \nis a low chance /<25%/ of measurable precipitation mainly east \nof I-79. Can not rule out sprinkles as far west as I-77 Sunday \nnight into Monday. \n\nThis won't be a washout in any area, but with mostly cloudy to an \novercast sky, temperatures during the day pull back into the mid and \nupper 60s save for areas west of I-77 who will see more\nsunshine. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nCome tuesday night, clouds associated with the nor'easter will \nbe out of our area. This allows a transitory H5 ridge axis to \nmove in returning dry and warmer weather. Thursday will be the \nwarmest day of the week with widespread 70s. Latest probs of \nmaxT 80F or greater continue to increase for northern WV, \nsoutheastern OH, and southwestern PA as they now reside between \n40-60%. This by no means is near record territory as benchmarks \nfor 1 October are still near 90F at most climate sites. Heat \nrisk at this juncture remains in the minor category. \n\nWe reside just south of a baroclinic zone which is along Lake Erie \ninto upstate New York placing us in the warm sector Friday and \nSaturday. The trend continues for dry weather, however a weak \nimpulse could traverse generating scattered showers.  For now, probs \nbetween 20-40% have been consistent in the warm sector.  With \nincreased moisture in the column, forecast highs pull back a little \non day 6 and 7 to the lower to mid 70s, while overnight lows near 60 \nare projected with above average confidence. \n\nFor locations who do not receive rainfall Sunday night or Monday \nthen none is expected the balance of September.",
